            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"One who fears the Lord, possesses divine insight, and practically applies God's truth to live righteously.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Proverbs 1:7\",\"Proverbs 9:10\",\"Matthew 7:24\",\"James 3:13-17\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Worldly or fleshly intellect, characterized by human philosophy, arrogance, and spiritual blindness.\",\"basis\":[\"1 Corinthians 1:18-25\",\"1 Corinthians 3:18-19\",\"Isaiah 5:21\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A christological symbol or personification of Christ himself (Wisdom incarnate).\",\"basis\":[\"Proverbs 8:22-31\",\"1 Corinthians 1:24\",\"1 Corinthians 1:30\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Gentile seekers of truth who recognize and worship the Messiah (the Magi).\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 2:1-12\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In biblical symbolism, the wise person is primarily contrasted with the fool. True wisdom is depicted not merely as intellectual capacity, but as moral and spiritual alignment with God. The New Testament further introduces a sharp dichotomy between the 'wise of this world' (a negative symbol of human pride) and those made wise by God.\"}",
